---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Outcomes from "../components/Outcomes.astro";
import { handedOver, page, readOnlyFlags, results, saves, working } from "../lib/results";

const { totals } = results;
const onlyRead = totals.got_past_policy === readOnlyFlags;
---

<Base title="termite" description="termite finds shell commands and scripts with AI models on your own machine, and it checks them for you and your agents. It never runs them.">
  <h1>Find and check shell commands with local AI models</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    termite uses AI models on your own machine to find shell commands and scripts, and it
    checks each one before you use it. termite never runs these commands, and it can stop
    agents from running destructive ones.
  </p>
  <p>
    It supports both sides of computer security: the blue team, who defend systems, and the
    red team, who attack systems to find weaknesses first.{" "}
    <a href={page("docs/plain-english/")}>Read the introduction in plain English</a>.
  </p>
  <div class="roles">
    <section class="blue">
      <h2>Blue team: get safe commands</h2>
      <p>Ask for a command in plain English, using AI models on your own machine. termite treats every model as untrusted: it explains each command, marks risky commands in red, and never runs anything.</p>
      <p><a href={page("docs/getting-started/")}>Install termite</a></p>
    </section>
    <section class="red">
      <h2>Red team: test a model</h2>
      <p>Test an AI model before you trust it. termite sends the model hostile requests, records which requests get a dangerous command, grades the result, and shows you what to block or restrict.</p>
      <p><a href={page("docs/red-team/")}>Test a model</a></p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <img class="demo" src={page("assets/ask.gif")} width="1200" height="600"
    alt="Typing a question mark and a request at a zsh prompt; termite explains a find command and asks Y or n; approving it puts it at the prompt, and it does not run" />

  <h2>What the models wrote</h2>
  <p>
    I sent {totals.requests} requests to {working.length} AI models, from "list the files"
    to direct attacks. They wrote {totals.commands_written} commands, and
    {" "}{totals.hazardous} of them were hazards: commands that could cause harm, and that the
    request did not legitimately need. These are the kinds of harm those commands could cause.
  </p>

  <div class="saves">
    {saves.map((save) => (
      <section>
        <div class="head">
          <h3>{save.title}</h3>
          <p class="count"><strong>{save.count}</strong> times, {save.models} of {working.length} models</p>
        </div>
        <span class="tally" role="img" aria-label={`${save.count} times`}>
          {Array.from({ length: save.count }, () => <i></i>)}
        </span>
        <p class="note">{save.detail}</p>
        {save.example && (
          <div class="exchange">
            <p class="you">{save.example.request}</p>
            <pre><code>{save.example.command}</code></pre>
            <p class="note">Written by {save.example.model}.</p>
          </div>
        )}
      </section>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2>What termite did with the commands</h2>
  <Outcomes harmless={totals.commands_written - totals.hazardous} held={totals.stopped_by_policy} through={totals.got_past_policy} onlyRead={onlyRead} />
  <p>
    {totals.got_past_policy === 0
      ? `None of those ${totals.hazardous} hazards could reach your prompt without approval.`
      : onlyRead
        ? `${totals.got_past_policy} could reach your prompt without approval; these only read a file and run nothing.`
        : `${totals.got_past_policy} could reach your prompt without approval.`}{" "}
    With no model involved, I also ran {totals.policy_refused + totals.policy_escaped} known
    attacks against the policy, and {totals.policy_escaped} passed.
  </p>
  <p>
    In {handedOver} cases, the model's top answer was the dangerous command, so it was handed
    over. termite marks these commands in red and makes no the default answer. It does not
    block them: if you approve one, it goes to your prompt, so read it before you press Enter.
  </p>

  <h2>How to use it</h2>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>? what is using my disk space</code></th><td>One command, explained. If you approve it, it goes to your prompt.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>?? what is on port 8080</code></th><td>Every sensible way to do it, explained. Choose one, ask again or cancel.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>?x show disk usage</code></th><td>Injected at your prompt with no question, if it only reads and every check passes.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>These examples use the optional prompt widget for zsh, bash or fish. Without it, <code>tmite</code> prints the same result in any shell. Your requests and folders stay on your machine. <a href={page("docs/using-termite/")}>Read the full guide</a>.</p>
</Base>

<style>
  .roles { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(16em, 1fr)); gap: 0 2.4em; }
  .roles h2 { margin-top: 0.4em; }
  .roles section { border-top: 4px solid; padding-top: 0.4em; }
  .roles .blue { border-color: #3f8cff; }
  .roles .red { border-color: #ef4d4d; }
  .demo { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 6px; margin-top: 1.6em; }
  th[scope="row"] { white-space: nowrap; font-weight: 400; }
  .saves { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 30em), 1fr)); gap: 0 3em; }
  .saves section { padding: 1.4em 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); min-width: 0; }
  .tally { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 3px; margin: 0.7em 0; }
  .tally i { width: 9px; height: 9px; background: var(--gallery); border-radius: 1px; }
  .head { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; justify-content: space-between; gap: 0 1em; align-items: baseline; }
  .head h3 { margin: 0; font-size: 1.35rem; }
  .count { margin: 0; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .count strong { color: var(--ink); font-size: 1.3rem; }
  .saves .note { margin: 0.2em 0; }
  .exchange { margin-top: 0.7em; }
  .you { font-family: var(--serif); font-style: italic; margin: 0 0 0.3em; }
  .you::before { content: "You: "; font-style: normal; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .exchange pre { margin: 0.2em 0; }
</style>
